# Carom — Playfield, paddles, obstacles, and ball

This file defines the geometry of the field and the objects in it, including how
the ball collides with the obstacles. All positions and sizes are in the
logical-pixel coordinate system from `specs/overview.md`, and every figure below
is exported from `src/constants.ts` under the name it is given here.

## Playfield

- The field spans the full `FIELD_W x FIELD_H` (`1280 x 720`) area.
- The top wall (`y = 0`) and the bottom wall (`y = FIELD_H`) are solid, and the
  ball reflects off them.
- The left and right edges are goals. A ball passing `x < 0` scores for player
  two (right); a ball passing `x > FIELD_W` scores for player one (left).
- A dashed center net is drawn at `x = NET_X` (`640`) for decoration and has no
  collision.

## Paddles

- Each paddle is a rounded bar `PADDLE_W` wide by `PADDLE_H` tall (`16 x 110`),
  with `PADDLE_HALF` (`55`) its half-height.
- The left (player one) paddle occupies `x` in `[P1_X0, P1_X1]` (`[48, 64]`), so
  `P1_X1` is its field-facing face. The right (player two or AI) paddle occupies
  `x` in `[P2_X0, P2_X1]` (`[1216, 1232]`), so `P2_X0` is its field-facing face.
- A paddle moves only vertically. Its center `y` is clamped to
  `[PADDLE_MIN_CY, PADDLE_MAX_CY]` (`[55, 665]`) so the paddle stays fully on the
  field.
- A paddle moves at `PADDLE_SPEED` (`720`) logical px/s while a movement action is
  held and is otherwise stationary. Its actual vertical velocity this frame drives
  the spin mechanic in `specs/balls.md`, so a paddle pinned against a bound has a
  velocity of `0` even while the action is held.

## Obstacles
{{#if (eq variant.slug "gyre")}}

Two obstacles sit in the field. Each is a rounded bar `OBSTACLE_W` wide by
`OBSTACLE_H` tall (`20 x 140`), placed mirror-symmetrically through the field
center `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` so neither side is favored, with base centers
`OBSTACLE_CENTERS[0] = (490, 220)` and `OBSTACLE_CENTERS[1] = (790, 500)`.
Unlike a plain field, these obstacles are live: they sway up and down and spin in
place. A spinning obstacle presents a tilted face, so the ball bounces off it at
angles that have nothing to do with the axis-aligned walls, reflecting off an
oriented rectangle. Reading these moving, rotating obstacles to bank shots is the
heart of the game.

### Moving, rotating obstacles

Each obstacle's pose is a function of an obstacle clock `t` (in seconds):

- Vertical oscillation. Each obstacle's center `x` is unchanged while its center
  `y` oscillates sinusoidally about its base center with amplitude
  `OBSTACLE_SWAY_AMP` (`80`) px and period `OBSTACLE_SWAY_PERIOD` (`3.6`) s. The
  two obstacles oscillate in anti-phase, so the layout stays point-symmetric about
  the field center and neither side is favored:
  - Obstacle A center:
    `(490, 220 + OBSTACLE_SWAY_AMP * sin(2*pi * t / OBSTACLE_SWAY_PERIOD))`.
  - Obstacle B center:
    `(790, 500 - OBSTACLE_SWAY_AMP * sin(2*pi * t / OBSTACLE_SWAY_PERIOD))`.
- Rotation. Each obstacle rotates continuously about its own center at the
  constant rate `OBSTACLE_SPIN_RATE` (`60 deg/s`, held in radians per second), in
  the same direction for both, with angle `theta(t) = OBSTACLE_SPIN_RATE * t`. At
  `t = 0` both obstacles are upright (long axis vertical), so the field starts
  upright and the motion grows from there.

At these values the obstacles stay clear of the top and bottom walls at all times,
even at full tilt, and the point-symmetry holds at every instant, so play is
balanced.

The obstacle clock `t` advances by the frame's delta time on every frame of a live
match, including during the pre-serve countdown, so the obstacles are already
moving when a ball is served. It is frozen while the game is paused, and it resets
to `0` at the start of each match, so every match opens with both obstacles
upright at their base centers. The obstacle motion is driven from this simulation
clock rather than wall-clock time, keeping it reproducible under the delta-time
integration `specs/balls.md` requires.

The obstacles are drawn rotated and shifted to match their current pose, in the
neon obstacle styling from `specs/overview.md`.

### Collision with oriented obstacles

Treat the ball as a circle of radius `BALL_R` (`11`) and each obstacle as a
rectangle with half-extents `(OBSTACLE_HW, OBSTACLE_HH)` (`10, 70`), the rounded
corners cosmetic, but that rectangle is oriented at the obstacle's current angle
`theta(t)` and centered at its current center `C = (cx, cy)`.

Each frame, after advancing the ball, resolve it against each obstacle at that
obstacle's current pose (`C`, `theta`):

1. Into the obstacle's local frame. Translate and un-rotate the ball center
   `P = (px, py)` into the obstacle's local axes (rotate by `-theta` about `C`):
   - `dx = px - cx`, `dy = py - cy`
   - `lx =  dx * cos(theta) + dy * sin(theta)`
   - `ly = -dx * sin(theta) + dy * cos(theta)`
   In this local frame the obstacle is an axis-aligned rectangle spanning
   `[-OBSTACLE_HW, OBSTACLE_HW] x [-OBSTACLE_HH, OBSTACLE_HH]`.
2. Closest point and overlap. Clamp to the rectangle:
   `qx = clamp(lx, -OBSTACLE_HW, OBSTACLE_HW)`,
   `qy = clamp(ly, -OBSTACLE_HH, OBSTACLE_HH)`. The ball overlaps the obstacle
   when the distance from `(lx, ly)` to `(qx, qy)` is less than `BALL_R`.
3. Local normal. If the ball center is outside the rectangle, the local contact
   normal is `normalize((lx - qx, ly - qy))`, a face normal for an edge hit and
   pointing out of a corner for a corner hit. If the ball center is inside the
   rectangle, use the axis of least penetration as the local normal (`+/-x` or
   `+/-y`).
4. Back to world. Rotate the local normal `(nlx, nly)` back by `+theta` to get the
   world normal `n`:
   - `nx = nlx * cos(theta) - nly * sin(theta)`
   - `ny = nlx * sin(theta) + nly * cos(theta)`
5. Reflect. If the ball is moving into the surface (`v . n < 0`), reflect the
   velocity about the world normal: `v' = v - 2 * (v . n) * n`. Speed is unchanged
   and spin is preserved (see `specs/balls.md`), so the spin keeps curving the
   ball after the bounce. Then push the ball out along `n` so it no longer
   overlaps, placing its center `BALL_R` off the contact face.

When `theta = 0` this reduces to the upright case: a hit on a vertical face flips
`vx` and a hit on a horizontal face flips `vy`. At any other angle the ball leaves
along the reflected direction off the tilted face, which is the distinctive
challenge of the game.

An obstacle's own motion changes only where and at what angle the ball is struck,
never its speed or spin, both of which are governed solely by `specs/balls.md`.
{{else}}

Two obstacles sit in the field, fixed in place and never moving. Each is a rounded
bar `OBSTACLE_W` wide by `OBSTACLE_H` tall (`20 x 140`), placed mirror-symmetrically
through the field center `(FIELD_CX, FIELD_CY)` so neither side is favored:

- Obstacle A is centered at `OBSTACLE_CENTERS[0]` (`490, 220`).
- Obstacle B is centered at `OBSTACLE_CENTERS[1]` (`790, 500`).

`OBSTACLES` holds the same two obstacles as axis-aligned rectangles, which is what
the collision below resolves against.

### Collision

Treat each obstacle as an axis-aligned rectangle with half-extents
`(OBSTACLE_HW, OBSTACLE_HH)` (`10, 70`), centered at its fixed center; the rounded
corners are cosmetic. When the ball strikes an obstacle, reflect the velocity
component normal to the face it hit, so a hit on a left or right (vertical) face
flips `vx` and a hit on a top or bottom (horizontal) face flips `vy`, then push
the ball out of the obstacle so it no longer overlaps.

An obstacle bounce leaves speed unchanged and preserves spin (see
`specs/balls.md`), so the spin keeps curving the ball after the bounce.
{{/if}}

## Ball

- Every ball is a circle of radius `BALL_R` (`11`).
- Serve speed is `SERVE_SPEED` (`520`) px/s. Each paddle hit multiplies a ball's
  speed by `SPEED_MULT` (`1.04`), up to the speed cap `SPEED_CAP` (`980`) px/s.
  Wall and obstacle bounces do not change speed.
- How many balls are in play, where they spawn, how they are served, what happens
  after a point, and the physics that move them are defined in `specs/balls.md`.
